# Cloudflare Pages equivalent of netlify.toml's [[redirects]] rules — see
# that file's comments for the reasoning. Kept in sync by hand during the
# Netlify -> Cloudflare Pages migration; once Netlify is fully retired,
# netlify.toml can go and this becomes the only copy.
#
# Confirmed live (not just from docs, which turned out to be misleading on
# this) that a Cloudflare Pages redirect does NOT reliably override a
# request whose path matches a real uploaded file — /render.yaml kept
# serving the real file instead of hitting its /404.html redirect below,
# same for /backend/*, /platform/*, /docs/*, /.github/*. Netlify's own
# default has the same limitation (a non-forced redirect is skipped when a
# real file matches, which is why netlify.toml needed force = true), so
# this isn't actually a regression versus Netlify's *documented* default —
# it just means the rules below were never a complete fix on their own.
#
# The real fix is build-cf-pages.mjs: it copies only the files this site
# needs to serve into _site/ (the actual deploy output), so backend/,
# platform/, docs/, .github/, render.yaml etc. are never uploaded as
# deployable assets in the first place — nothing here for a redirect to
# lose a race against. The rules below stay as defense in depth (in case
# a stray file with one of these names ever ends up in the real output)
# rather than as the primary protection.

/find-talent.html  /for-employers.html#categories  301

/backend/*   /404.html  404
/platform/*  /404.html  404
/docs/*      /404.html  404
/.github/*   /404.html  404
/.git/*      /404.html  404
/render.yaml /404.html  404

/*  /404.html  404
